WHY THE LICENSE CHANGED — INTENT WAS ALWAYS PROPRIETARY
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Relayon was in active early development. During rapid prototyping,
a boilerplate MIT `LICENSE` file was added to the repository by
oversight — simply because it was the fastest "normal default"
available, without any deliberate licensing decision having been
made.

**Relayon was never intended to be released under MIT.** From
day one, the project was designed as a proprietary, source-visible
product — the kind of software you may freely use and self-host,
but may not modify, fork, rebrand, or resell.

The MIT file was a development-phase placeholder that was not
replaced in time before the v1.5 tag was publicly accessible.

Effective **November 20, 2026**, the licensing is corrected and
formalized:

- All versions from **v1.6 onward** — and all future versions —
  are licensed under the RELAYON PROPRIETARY SOURCE-VISIBLE
  LICENSE. All rights reserved by Zain Shah.
- v1.6 through v3.5 were **never made public** and carry no MIT
  grant. They are fully proprietary.
- The mistake for v1.0–v1.5 is documented here for transparency.
  Due to the irrevocable nature of the MIT License, this
  declaration does not and cannot revoke the MIT rights of anyone
  who obtained a copy of those versions while the repository was
  public.

